  "response": "# Solution\n\nUsing Bayes' theorem with:\n- Prevalence: P(D) = 1/500 = 0.002\n- Sensitivity: P(+|D) = 0.98\n- Specificity: P(–|¬D) = 0.95, so false positive rate P(+|¬D) = 0.05\n\n**Step 1: True positives**\nP(D and +) = 0.002 × 0.98 = 0.00196\n\n**Step 2: False positives**\nP(¬D and +) = 0.998 × 0.05 = 0.0499\n\n**Step 3: Total positives**\nP(+) = 0.00196 + 0.0499 = 0.05186\n\n**Step 4: Posterior probability (PPV)**\nP(D|+) = 0.00196 / 0.05186 ≈ 0.0377944\n\nConverting to a percentage: **3.78%**\n\nThis illustrates the classic base-rate effect: even with a fairly accurate test, because the condition is rare, most positive results are false positives — only about 1 in 26 people who test positive actually has the condition.\n\nFINAL ANSWER: 3.78",
